WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.680
Two children have malformed cortices.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.520
One is small, with its layers in order.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.180
The other is thick and nearly smooth.

4
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.220
Which step failed in each?

5
00:00:14.183 --> 00:00:16.263
Building a cortex takes two jobs.

6
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:21.243
Radial glia at the ventricle divide, to make more of themselves and then neurons.

7
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:25.427
Then each neuron climbs a glial fiber to its layer.

8
00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:28.387
So too few can be made, or they can stall.

9
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:31.097
The first job multiplies.

10
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:34.157
Early on, each division makes two progenitors.

11
00:00:35.097 --> 00:00:36.517
So every round doubles the pool.

12
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.270
Switch one round early, and the pool is halved.

13
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.390
Switch two rounds early, and it falls to a quarter.

14
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:45.790
Now a subtler fault.

15
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:51.190
Suppose each division has a one in ten chance of coming out neurogenic, for that

16
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.190
round only.

17
00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:55.843
Predict. Let the pool run ten rounds.

18
00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:58.503
How big is it then, compared with normal?

19
00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:10.043
About 60 Each neurogenic division is one progenitor short.

20
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:13.283
So each round falls 5 percent short of doubling.

21
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:17.347
Over ten rounds, that costs two-fifths of the pool.

22
00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:19.427
Small faults compound.

23
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.847
The second job does not multiply.

24
00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:28.087
A neuron that stalls takes no descendants with it.

25
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.760
At the bedside, size is read with a tape measure.

26
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.520
The cutoff for microcephaly is two standard deviations below the mean.

27
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.890
On a rough sphere model, a head at the cutoff holds a brain only about a fifth

28
00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.250
smaller. That compares volumes.

29
00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.410
It does not count neurons, or lost divisions.

30
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:54.613
Children with ASPM mutations often have far smaller heads, many standard deviations

31
00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:55.613
below the mean.

32
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:01.047
In a bell-shaped population, about two babies in a hundred fall below the cutoff.

33
00:02:01.927 --> 00:02:04.187
A small head is a sign, not a diagnosis.

34
00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:06.767
Here is the one idea.

35
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:09.007
Size counts the doublings.

36
00:02:09.007 --> 00:02:11.007
Layering records the journey.

37
00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:14.447
Small with orderly layers points to production.

38
00:02:15.487 --> 00:02:18.467
Near-normal size with disordered layers points to migration.

39
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:23.097
It is a first question, not a diagnosis.

40
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.960
So when a cortex is malformed as it is built, ask first which job failed.
